1. Implement a simple rate limiter using Spring's `RateLimiter` class to limit the number of requests made to the system within a certain time window. This can be achieved by creating a custom filter that wraps the existing request handler and checks the rate limit before allowing the request to proceed. For example, you can create a new file called `ThrottlingFilter.java` in the `com.example.filters` package with the following code:
```java
import org.springframework.http.HttpHeaders;
import org.springframework.http.HttpStatus;
import org.springframework.web.filter.GenericFilterBean;

public class ThrottlingFilter extends GenericFilterBean {
    private static final int MAX_REQUESTS_PER_MINUTE = 10;
    private static final long TIME_WINDOW_IN_MS = 60 * 1000;

    @Override
    public void doFilter(ServletRequest request, ServletResponse response, FilterChain chain) throws IOException, ServletException {
        // Get the current timestamp
        long now = System.currentTimeMillis();

        // Check if we've exceeded the rate limit
        int requestsInWindow = getRequestsInWindow(now);
        if (requestsInWindow >= MAX_REQUESTS_PER_MINUTE) {
            ((HttpServletResponse) response).setStatus(HttpStatus.TOO_MANY_REQUESTS);
            return;
        }

        // Proceed with the request
        chain.doFilter(request, response);
    }

    private int getRequestsInWindow(long now) {
        // Get the list of requests from the database or cache
        List<Request> requests = getRequestList(now - TIME_WINDOW_IN_MS);

        // Count the number of requests in the window
        int count = 0;
        for (Request request : requests) {
            if (request.getTimestamp() >= now - TIME_WINDOW_IN_MS) {
                count++;
            }
        }

        return count;
    }
}
```
This filter will limit the number of requests made to the system within a minute to 10 requests per minute.

